**Title: The Last Route**

In the heart of the city, where neon signs flickered and the hum of traffic never ceased, there stood a small, rusted delivery robot named Z-7. Once, it had been the pride of the city’s logistics network, zipping through streets with precision and purpose. But now, it was retired, its once-bright blue paint dulled by years of use, its sensors dimmed by time.

Z-7 had delivered everything from groceries to medical supplies, from birthday cakes to urgent documents. It had known every alley, every shortcut, every corner of the city. But as the years passed, the city had changed. New buildings rose where old ones had stood. Streets were re-routed. And people—people had forgotten it.

One evening, as the sun dipped below the skyline and the city settled into its quiet hum, Z-7 stirred. A soft chime echoed through its circuits, a signal it hadn’t heard in years. It was a request for delivery, sent from a single address in the old district. The address was familiar. It had once been a home, a place where a family had lived, and where Z-7 had delivered a birthday cake for a young girl named Lila.


